{
  "body_human_readable": "**Archimedes of Syracuse: A Titan of Ancient Science and Mathematics**\n\nArchimedes of Syracuse, who lived from approximately 287 to 212 BC, is widely regarded as one of the most brilliant minds of classical antiquity. A Greek mathematician, physicist, engineer, astronomer, and inventor, his work was so influential that he is often called the \"Father of Mathematics.\" Though few details of his life are known, he is considered one of the leading scientists of his time and one of the greatest mathematicians of all time.\n\nBorn in the Sicilian city of Syracuse, which was then a Greek colony, Archimedes' father was an astronomer named Phidias. It is believed that he received his formal education in Alexandria, Egypt, a center of learning in the ancient world.\n\n**A Legacy of Innovation and Discovery**\n\nArchimedes' contributions span a remarkable range of fields. He is perhaps most famous for his \"Eureka!\" moment, when he discovered the principle of buoyancy while taking a bath. This principle, now known as Archimedes' principle, states that an object submerged in a fluid experiences an upward buoyant force equal to the weight of the fluid it displaces. This fundamental concept explains why ships float.\n\nHis ingenuity as an inventor is exemplified by the Archimedes screw, a device for raising water that is still in use today for irrigation. He also created complex systems of levers and pulleys capable of moving large objects, including ships. During the Roman siege of Syracuse, he devised a variety of war machines to defend his city, earning him a formidable reputation.\n\nIn the realm of mathematics, Archimedes made profound contributions. He developed a method for calculating an approximation of pi (\u03c0) and was one of the first to apply mathematical principles to physical phenomena. He also did extensive work on the properties of statics and hydrostatics, including proving the law of the lever. He found that the volume of a sphere is two-thirds the volume of a cylinder that encloses it, a discovery he valued so highly that he requested a sphere and cylinder be placed on his tomb.\n\nArchimedes died during the capture of Syracuse by the Romans. Despite orders from the Roman general, Marcellus, that he should not be harmed, he was killed by a Roman soldier. One account suggests he was so engrossed in a mathematical problem that he refused to leave his work.",
